WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.570
VICTOR DAVIS HANSON:
Are you a history major?

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:02.070
NICK: I am.

00:00:02.070 --> 00:00:03.960
I'm a history and applied
math double major.

00:00:03.960 --> 00:00:04.785
And a math?

00:00:04.785 --> 00:00:05.535
Yes, sir.

00:00:05.535 --> 00:00:06.660
What do you want to do?

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:07.980
I'm not quite sure.

00:00:07.980 --> 00:00:09.685
Well, academia is not lucrative.

00:00:09.685 --> 00:00:10.185
Right.

00:00:10.185 --> 00:00:12.255
Well, I'm not
interested in academia.

00:00:12.255 --> 00:00:14.760
I farmed, and I had three kids,
and I lived in an old house.

00:00:14.760 --> 00:00:16.350
And I was making $25,000.

00:00:16.350 --> 00:00:18.420
And something told me--

00:00:18.420 --> 00:00:20.130
one day, I spoke to
a business group.

00:00:20.130 --> 00:00:22.110
And they said,
you're either going

00:00:22.110 --> 00:00:24.240
to have to write books
or speak or do something

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.680
because you're headed
toward poverty.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:27.180
Wow.

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:28.847
That was really
shocking that somebody'd

00:00:28.847 --> 00:00:30.075
tell me that in my 30s.

00:00:30.075 --> 00:00:30.735
Wow.

00:00:30.735 --> 00:00:31.443
And he was right.

00:00:31.443 --> 00:00:33.427
So I started writing books.

00:00:33.427 --> 00:00:35.010
It was also because
of farming, right?

00:00:35.010 --> 00:00:36.930
Because someone came in
and wanted to publish

00:00:36.930 --> 00:00:38.995
your doctoral thesis, right?

00:00:38.995 --> 00:00:39.850
Yeah.

00:00:39.850 --> 00:00:41.130
Yeah, it was very odd.

00:00:41.130 --> 00:00:43.980
A man named Emilio Gabba--
he's a very famous classicist--

00:00:43.980 --> 00:00:46.290
he just happened
to be in Berkeley.

00:00:46.290 --> 00:00:47.190
And I was farming.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:50.040
I had finished my PhD
thesis three years earlier.

00:00:50.040 --> 00:00:52.950
And I got a letter
out of the blue.

00:00:52.950 --> 00:00:55.425
He was a visiting
Sather Professor,

00:00:55.425 --> 00:00:57.930
which was one of the
top professorships.

00:00:57.930 --> 00:01:00.240
A very nice letter.

00:01:00.240 --> 00:01:03.000
He said, we're from the
University of Padua,

00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.019
and we would like to
publish your thesis.

00:01:05.019 --> 00:01:08.880
And I was on a tractor when
my wife gave me the letter.

00:01:08.880 --> 00:01:12.520
And he said, you don't
have to do anything.

00:01:12.520 --> 00:01:13.890
And then they published it.

00:01:13.890 --> 00:01:15.570
And it went to John Keegan.

00:01:15.570 --> 00:01:18.300
And the next thing I knew, he
read it and liked it and said,

00:01:18.300 --> 00:01:22.440
if you write another book,
I will write the foreword.

00:01:22.440 --> 00:01:24.540
So I wrote a book called
The Western Way of War

00:01:24.540 --> 00:01:26.790
about what it was like to
fight in the ancient world.

00:01:26.790 --> 00:01:29.220
And the next thing I know,
he wrote the foreword.

00:01:29.220 --> 00:01:30.730
And it sold very well.

00:01:30.730 --> 00:01:34.530
So for somebody in
their early and mid-30s,

00:01:34.530 --> 00:01:36.510
it was kind of unusual.

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.520
Because I farmed for 20 years.

00:01:38.520 --> 00:01:40.170
And then I would
get off the tractor

00:01:40.170 --> 00:01:44.620
and go in and teach 25 miles
away, sometimes twice a day.

00:01:44.620 --> 00:01:46.725
So it was kind of a hectic life.

00:01:46.725 --> 00:01:48.373
I kind of like that, though.

00:01:48.373 --> 00:01:50.790
The best teachers I've ever
had in high school or whatever

00:01:50.790 --> 00:01:52.957
have always done something
else other than teaching.

00:01:52.957 --> 00:01:53.850
Yeah, you have to.

00:01:53.850 --> 00:01:55.530
But it was very schizophrenic.

00:01:55.530 --> 00:02:00.900
Because all of the things
that I was supposed to do at

00:02:00.900 --> 00:02:03.930
the campus was antithetical to
what I was doing in farming.

00:02:03.930 --> 00:02:06.240
You were supposed to be
very careful what you said.

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:08.460
And it was very relaxed.

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:12.840
And there were some pretty wild
characters out in the Central

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:19.140
Valley on farming, stealing
your water or other farmers

00:02:19.140 --> 00:02:24.390
that would fight over a road
or who had the right to turn,

00:02:24.390 --> 00:02:26.100
or you're making
your turns to big.

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:28.110
And it was pretty blunt.

00:02:28.110 --> 00:02:30.960
And then you'd go up there and
it was such a different change

00:02:30.960 --> 00:02:31.485
every day.

00:02:31.485 --> 00:02:33.460
You kind of had to be
John Wayne a little bit.

00:02:33.460 --> 00:02:33.590
Yeah.

00:02:33.590 --> 00:02:35.195
I wrote a couple books about it.

00:02:35.195 --> 00:02:37.440
So speaking of books,
you've got one coming out

00:02:37.440 --> 00:02:39.220
very soon on citizenship.

00:02:39.220 --> 00:02:40.470
It's called The Dying Citizen.

00:02:40.470 --> 00:02:43.530
And I'm an American
citizen, right?

00:02:43.530 --> 00:02:44.430
I was born a citizen.

00:02:44.430 --> 00:02:46.620
I didn't have to do anything
special to get that.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.075
Why is this idea important?

00:02:49.075 --> 00:02:49.950
Well, let me ask you.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:52.380
Are you shocked that
2 million people

00:02:52.380 --> 00:02:54.742
are scheduled to walk
across the Southern border?

00:02:54.742 --> 00:02:55.522
Absolutely.

00:02:55.522 --> 00:02:59.010
And without any ID,
no background check?

00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:00.510
And the first thing
they do is going

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:02.400
to break federal
immigration law.

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:04.350
And the second thing
they're going to do

00:03:04.350 --> 00:03:07.092
is break federal immigration
law by residing here illegally.

00:03:07.092 --> 00:03:08.550
And the third is,
probably, they're

00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:12.510
going to have ID that is
not legal to continue to.

00:03:12.510 --> 00:03:14.700
And this is all at a
time when all of us

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.190
are supposed to be vaccinated.

00:03:17.190 --> 00:03:20.410
Or we're looking at
pictures of people landing--

00:03:20.410 --> 00:03:24.360
and we all support
legitimate refugees coming--

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:28.530
but they are arriving in
bases without any requirement

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:30.630
as foreign nationals
to be vaccinated

00:03:30.630 --> 00:03:32.460
by troops who have
a deadline that they

00:03:32.460 --> 00:03:36.060
have to be vaccinated.

00:03:36.060 --> 00:03:38.880
Or where I live, we have
school board elections now

00:03:38.880 --> 00:03:42.840
where people who are not
US citizens can vote.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:48.300
Or all of the traditional
privileges of citizenship--

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:50.400
only citizens could
leave the United States

00:03:50.400 --> 00:03:51.510
at will with a passport.

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.980
That no longer applies.

00:03:52.980 --> 00:03:56.160
Citizens usually were
the people who were

00:03:56.160 --> 00:03:57.900
eligible for state services.

00:03:57.900 --> 00:03:59.280
That no longer applies.

00:03:59.280 --> 00:04:02.010
Citizens alone
could hold office.

00:04:02.010 --> 00:04:04.020
That is still there.

00:04:04.020 --> 00:04:06.750
It's the only one I can think
of that distinguishes you

00:04:06.750 --> 00:04:10.620
and I from a resident,
whether legal or not.

00:04:10.620 --> 00:04:14.550
They can vote, in some
cases, serve in the military.

00:04:14.550 --> 00:04:17.709
So I was curious about all this.

00:04:17.709 --> 00:04:21.300
And I tried to find out
why that was historically

00:04:21.300 --> 00:04:23.190
and what was so good
about citizenship.

00:04:23.190 --> 00:04:25.890
Because I would bump in where
I live-- most of the people

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:28.470
I bumped into out in rural
Fresno County were not

00:04:28.470 --> 00:04:29.352
citizens.

00:04:29.352 --> 00:04:30.810
And I had no idea
whether they were

00:04:30.810 --> 00:04:32.520
legal or illegal residents.

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.820
It was chaotic.

00:04:33.820 --> 00:04:36.180
There were castes, as it were.

00:04:36.180 --> 00:04:38.340
And I didn't have a
commonality with them

00:04:38.340 --> 00:04:41.160
because they just came
across the border.

00:04:41.160 --> 00:04:43.330
And then, when you look
at classical texts,

00:04:43.330 --> 00:04:46.227
the first thing you
see is a middle class.

00:04:46.227 --> 00:04:48.060
They're not envious of
the rich, and they're

00:04:48.060 --> 00:04:50.730
not dependent on
government, as the poor are.

00:04:50.730 --> 00:04:53.820
And they're not always
cajoling, as the rich are,

00:04:53.820 --> 00:04:56.940
to get quid pro quo concessions.

00:04:56.940 --> 00:04:58.950
Aristotle talks about
the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:04:58.950 --> 00:05:02.930
And yet, if you look at middle
class income, college, tuition

00:05:02.930 --> 00:05:09.250
debt, credit card debt, the
middle class is beleaguered.

00:05:09.250 --> 00:05:11.000
Especially under
globalization, the people

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.500
in the interior of
the country, they

00:05:12.500 --> 00:05:15.920
didn't have the skills that
could be internationalized.

00:05:15.920 --> 00:05:18.530
And what was weird about
it is it has repercussions.

00:05:18.530 --> 00:05:20.480
The date people
are getting married

00:05:20.480 --> 00:05:23.360
has gone from about 23 to 29.

00:05:23.360 --> 00:05:28.280
The first child is
from about 24 to 31.

00:05:28.280 --> 00:05:30.500
The date when people buy
their first home-- so we're

00:05:30.500 --> 00:05:34.490
creating a prolonged, I
could use the term pajama boy

00:05:34.490 --> 00:05:37.160
or life of Julia,
those commercials we

00:05:37.160 --> 00:05:40.220
saw during Obamacare--
prolonged adolescence.

00:05:40.220 --> 00:05:42.530
And then I also notice that,
in this organic process,

00:05:42.530 --> 00:05:45.080
it was almost like the
migrations of the late empire.

00:05:45.080 --> 00:05:47.300
People were just
coming and going.

00:05:47.300 --> 00:05:51.410
And there wasn't a sacred
space, a familiar landscape,

00:05:51.410 --> 00:05:54.740
where people had a
chance to reiterate

00:05:54.740 --> 00:05:58.130
their customs, their
traditions, their familiarity

00:05:58.130 --> 00:05:58.940
with citizens.

00:05:58.940 --> 00:06:02.540
And yet that had been a hallmark
of classical Greek and Roman

00:06:02.540 --> 00:06:05.360
citizenship, to the point people
went to war if their borders

00:06:05.360 --> 00:06:07.670
were impaired, say, in Greece.

00:06:07.670 --> 00:06:09.620
And yet we've lost that element.

00:06:09.620 --> 00:06:12.650
Then the big bane of
citizenship was always

00:06:12.650 --> 00:06:17.270
a pre-civilizational,
pre-modern tribalism.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:19.400
So you and I have
nothing in common

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:22.520
as US citizens,
only a superficial,

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:24.530
say, we look alike.

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:26.390
And we're going
to identify first

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:28.770
with our racial or ethnic tribe.

00:06:28.770 --> 00:06:31.670
In other words, race is
essential, not incidental,

00:06:31.670 --> 00:06:32.570
to who we are.

00:06:32.570 --> 00:06:36.320
And that leads nowhere
but to Rwanda or Iraq

00:06:36.320 --> 00:06:37.730
or the former Yugoslavia.

00:06:37.730 --> 00:06:39.480
And yet that's what
we're doing right now.

00:06:39.480 --> 00:06:42.590
We're regressing
back to 1840, 1850.

00:06:42.590 --> 00:06:45.120
And we're all in
this woke movement.

00:06:45.120 --> 00:06:47.330
And then the second half of
the book, really quickly,

00:06:47.330 --> 00:06:50.090
is that there's an elite
concentrated effort

00:06:50.090 --> 00:06:52.430
to dismantle citizenship.

00:06:52.430 --> 00:06:56.480
And you can see it
with the rejection

00:06:56.480 --> 00:07:00.860
of the word "equality," which
means equality of opportunity,

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:02.240
replaced by equity--

00:07:02.240 --> 00:07:04.070
an equality of
result. Government

00:07:04.070 --> 00:07:08.990
is going to redistribute
income and advantage,

00:07:08.990 --> 00:07:10.650
often by race and gender.

00:07:10.650 --> 00:07:11.910
So what are they doing?

00:07:11.910 --> 00:07:15.320
How do they facilitate
that agenda?

00:07:15.320 --> 00:07:18.110
One thing is we have a
permanent administrative state.

00:07:18.110 --> 00:07:18.845
We always have.

00:07:18.845 --> 00:07:21.950
It started, really, during
the Depression with the New

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:23.240
Deal and the Great Society.

00:07:23.240 --> 00:07:26.210
But now about 45% of Americans--

00:07:26.210 --> 00:07:29.900
40% work for the state,
local, or federal government.

00:07:29.900 --> 00:07:31.070
Then we have evolutionaries.

00:07:31.070 --> 00:07:33.260
You probably have seen
these, or you know them--

00:07:33.260 --> 00:07:36.980
academics, lawyers, activists.

00:07:36.980 --> 00:07:38.900
And they don't like
the Constitution.

00:07:38.900 --> 00:07:41.690
That's a big issue
here at Hillsdale.

00:07:41.690 --> 00:07:43.160
So what do they want to do?

00:07:43.160 --> 00:07:44.810
They want to evolve beyond it.

00:07:44.810 --> 00:07:48.380
So we're going to get rid
of 180-year filibuster--

00:07:48.380 --> 00:07:52.200
not in the Constitution, but
it's a custom and tradition.

00:07:52.200 --> 00:07:55.055
The electoral
college is 233 years.

00:07:55.055 --> 00:07:56.420
Let's junk it.

00:07:56.420 --> 00:07:59.030
The idea that a state like
Michigan or California

00:07:59.030 --> 00:08:02.090
sets their own voting laws,
even for national elections

00:08:02.090 --> 00:08:05.150
unless the federal government
wants to come in for women's

00:08:05.150 --> 00:08:07.280
right, suffrage, or
18-year-old vote.

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:09.320
But we're going to
try to junk that.

00:08:09.320 --> 00:08:13.490
150-year-old, nine-person,
Supreme Court.

00:08:13.490 --> 00:08:15.830
60-year-old 50
states in the Union.

00:08:15.830 --> 00:08:16.460
I could go on.

00:08:16.460 --> 00:08:20.330
But when the system doesn't work
for the progressive mindset,

00:08:20.330 --> 00:08:23.180
then either change the
demography or change the rules

00:08:23.180 --> 00:08:27.140
or change the whole
constitutional apparatus

00:08:27.140 --> 00:08:29.485
by which we make laws
and elect officials.

00:08:29.485 --> 00:08:30.860
And then, finally,
in the book, I

00:08:30.860 --> 00:08:34.220
have a danger that I'm
really worried about.

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:35.960
And that's globalization.

00:08:35.960 --> 00:08:38.510
It's an ancient idea
of cosmopolitanism,

00:08:38.510 --> 00:08:41.460
which is a Greek word for
"citizen of the world."

00:08:41.460 --> 00:08:44.930
But a lot of our elites,
our bicoastal elites,

00:08:44.930 --> 00:08:48.890
feel that whether it's
the Davos Great Reset

00:08:48.890 --> 00:08:51.320
project or the
International Criminal Court

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:56.060
or a UN Commission
on Human Rights,

00:08:56.060 --> 00:08:57.860
should trump US sovereignty.

00:08:57.860 --> 00:08:59.730
They feel we're
sort of an anomaly.

00:08:59.730 --> 00:09:02.750
We're a weird people that have
Second Amendment or abortion

00:09:02.750 --> 00:09:06.300
laws, banning types of abortion.

00:09:06.300 --> 00:09:07.550
We need to get with it.

00:09:07.550 --> 00:09:10.730
And you can see that when Antony
Blinken, our current Secretary

00:09:10.730 --> 00:09:13.680
of State, says that the UN
should come in and investigate

00:09:13.680 --> 00:09:14.180
us for--

00:09:14.180 --> 00:09:17.360
I could go on, but that's
a very dangerous idea.

00:09:17.360 --> 00:09:20.750
Globalization was wonderful
in giving Western consumer

00:09:20.750 --> 00:09:23.420
products, medicines
all over the world.

00:09:23.420 --> 00:09:26.450
But the idea that you
would take the next step

00:09:26.450 --> 00:09:30.650
and harmonize political
norms and institutions

00:09:30.650 --> 00:09:33.230
outside the United States
that are far inferior

00:09:33.230 --> 00:09:34.510
is really dangerous.

00:09:34.510 --> 00:09:35.010
Right.

00:09:35.010 --> 00:09:36.950
And I think there is
some sort of, perhaps,

00:09:36.950 --> 00:09:39.680
a loathing, I guess, of American
exceptionalism or something.

00:09:39.680 --> 00:09:39.790
They do.

00:09:39.790 --> 00:09:41.373
And there's a desire
to change things.

00:09:41.373 --> 00:09:43.340
Where do you think
that comes from?

00:09:43.340 --> 00:09:45.350
Well, Barack Obama,
you remember,

00:09:45.350 --> 00:09:47.150
he had said on two
occasions, I believe

00:09:47.150 --> 00:09:49.427
we're exceptional like
the Greeks or the British.

00:09:49.427 --> 00:09:51.260
In other words, everybody
believes they are.

00:09:51.260 --> 00:09:53.750
But he made no
effort to quantify

00:09:53.750 --> 00:09:57.110
that in a dispassionate,
empirical fashion.

00:09:57.110 --> 00:09:59.220
Do other countries
have due process?

00:09:59.220 --> 00:10:00.880
Do they have habeas corpus?

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:02.200
Do they have a free market?

00:10:02.200 --> 00:10:04.540
Do they have rights
of inheritance?

00:10:04.540 --> 00:10:08.200
Do they have a rational
system of adjudicating

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:10.360
natural phenomena or
scientific inquiry?

00:10:10.360 --> 00:10:11.980
Do they have gender equity?

00:10:11.980 --> 00:10:13.540
Do they have minority rights?

00:10:13.540 --> 00:10:14.410
No.

00:10:14.410 --> 00:10:18.340
Most of the 198 nations
in the world do not.

00:10:18.340 --> 00:10:20.350
But this hatred of
America, I think

00:10:20.350 --> 00:10:26.320
it comes from, to be quite
frank, from envy and jealousy.

00:10:26.320 --> 00:10:31.210
Because this country only
has 330 million people.

00:10:31.210 --> 00:10:34.330
It's not nearly as big as China,
either area-wise or population.

00:10:34.330 --> 00:10:36.560
It has 1.4 billion.

00:10:36.560 --> 00:10:38.560
Why is it so powerful?

00:10:38.560 --> 00:10:40.270
Why does it have
the largest economy?

00:10:40.270 --> 00:10:44.020
Why does one US worker at this
late date in American history

00:10:44.020 --> 00:10:47.350
still produce 40% more
goods and services

00:10:47.350 --> 00:10:49.840
than three of his
Chinese counterparts?

00:10:49.840 --> 00:10:52.030
And when they look
at popular culture,

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:55.130
why are American music
all over the world?

00:10:55.130 --> 00:10:57.760
And they think, just
stop it, you guys.

00:10:57.760 --> 00:11:01.520
So they never make the
next step and say, well,

00:11:01.520 --> 00:11:03.460
maybe we should have
a bill of rights,

00:11:03.460 --> 00:11:05.860
or maybe we should have
a free market economy,

00:11:05.860 --> 00:11:08.200
or maybe we shouldn't
have government so large

00:11:08.200 --> 00:11:09.445
in our lives.

00:11:09.445 --> 00:11:11.110
Sort of like a
regression to the mean.

00:11:11.110 --> 00:11:13.152
Instead of rising up,
they're going to just bring

00:11:13.152 --> 00:11:14.740
us down is sort of the idea.

00:11:14.740 --> 00:11:19.990
I think Aristotle and Hesiod
and Tocqueville, all of these--

00:11:19.990 --> 00:11:24.040
they all postulate the envy and
jealousy are the most powerful

00:11:24.040 --> 00:11:24.790
of emotions.

00:11:24.790 --> 00:11:26.920
And they can be
very destructive.

00:11:26.920 --> 00:11:28.600
Hesiod said there's a good envy.

00:11:28.600 --> 00:11:31.420
And that makes you see that
guy over there and say,

00:11:31.420 --> 00:11:32.710
he's got a Mercedes.

00:11:32.710 --> 00:11:34.120
This is the American attitude.

00:11:34.120 --> 00:11:35.680
You go over there
and you say to him,

00:11:35.680 --> 00:11:36.930
how did you get that Mercedes?

00:11:36.930 --> 00:11:38.740
He said, well, I
sold real estate.

00:11:38.740 --> 00:11:40.900
Wow, I want to be that.

00:11:40.900 --> 00:11:44.800
The British or especially the
continental European attitude

00:11:44.800 --> 00:11:46.720
or most places is
I'm going to go

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:48.003
kick the door in on that guy.

00:11:48.003 --> 00:11:49.420
He must have done
something wrong.

00:11:49.420 --> 00:11:52.150
And I'm a better person,
and I don't have one.

00:11:52.150 --> 00:11:55.840
And that's why we're in a crisis
now with the woke movement

00:11:55.840 --> 00:11:57.940
because that's
one of its tenets,

00:11:57.940 --> 00:11:59.200
that the system is unfair.

00:11:59.200 --> 00:12:00.340
It always was.

00:12:00.340 --> 00:12:03.070
1776 is not our founding date.

00:12:03.070 --> 00:12:04.300
It was always racist.

00:12:04.300 --> 00:12:06.880
It was always classist.

00:12:06.880 --> 00:12:10.550
And we need a complete
year-zero recalibration.

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:11.050
Yeah.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:12.467
So I'm taking a
class, and we were

00:12:12.467 --> 00:12:14.080
discussing the fall of Rome.

00:12:14.080 --> 00:12:16.630
And I think, since
Rome is the model,

00:12:16.630 --> 00:12:18.393
I suppose, for citizenship
that we adopted

00:12:18.393 --> 00:12:20.560
and everything, I find it
also interesting, in Rome,

00:12:20.560 --> 00:12:23.545
citizenship was not birthright
citizenship like it is here.

00:12:23.545 --> 00:12:25.930
There was an essay
by a French scholar

00:12:25.930 --> 00:12:29.560
that postulated 240 causes
to the fall of Rome,

00:12:29.560 --> 00:12:33.100
whether it was inflation or too
much military spending or too

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:36.070
little or Gibbon
Christianity that

00:12:36.070 --> 00:12:39.130
destroyed the martial
audacity of the Legion--

00:12:39.130 --> 00:12:39.950
whatever it was.

00:12:39.950 --> 00:12:44.200
But I think one of the more
pertinent or compelling reasons

00:12:44.200 --> 00:12:50.290
was as it expanded from a
republic to a Mediterranean

00:12:50.290 --> 00:12:54.940
empire to a global
empire, pretty soon,

00:12:54.940 --> 00:12:58.755
tribalization, regionalization,
and the system broke down.

00:12:58.755 --> 00:13:00.130
And that's what's
very scary now,

00:13:00.130 --> 00:13:04.210
because we're seeing it in
a variety of manifestations.

00:13:04.210 --> 00:13:05.710
We're having a red
and blue state.

00:13:05.710 --> 00:13:08.560
People are self-selecting
geographically.

00:13:08.560 --> 00:13:10.960
And we're not having this
idea of the melting pot.

00:13:10.960 --> 00:13:14.440
It's been rejected in favor
of the salad bowl and, now,

00:13:14.440 --> 00:13:16.660
the woke bowl,
that says you have

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:18.700
to be racist to stop racism.

00:13:18.700 --> 00:13:20.290
It's an absurd idea.

00:13:20.290 --> 00:13:23.770
And the idea that we're
going to check our DNA

00:13:23.770 --> 00:13:28.930
to find ethnic vitaes, look
how ridiculous it becomes.

00:13:28.930 --> 00:13:31.630
Ward Churchill is a Native
American, Elizabeth Warren

00:13:31.630 --> 00:13:33.220
with high cheekbones.

00:13:33.220 --> 00:13:35.470
Rachel Dolezal is
suddenly Black.

00:13:35.470 --> 00:13:37.990
Shaun King is as white as I am.

00:13:37.990 --> 00:13:41.650
And it's almost the reverse
pattern of Jim Crow.

00:13:41.650 --> 00:13:43.900
When I was a young
kid, you would

00:13:43.900 --> 00:13:46.000
hear stories of
African-Americans

00:13:46.000 --> 00:13:48.430
who were subject
to enormous racism,

00:13:48.430 --> 00:13:49.990
and they were mixed heritage.

00:13:49.990 --> 00:13:52.660
And they would,
say, pass for white.

00:13:52.660 --> 00:13:55.090
Now we have people
that are passing.

00:13:55.090 --> 00:13:58.180
And that should tell us
something, that the system then

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:00.460
is not racially blind.

00:14:00.460 --> 00:14:02.530
And it's what
Professor Kendi says,

00:14:02.530 --> 00:14:07.660
that it's OK to be racist
to rectify prior racism.

00:14:07.660 --> 00:14:12.850
But the problem is we're
150 years from slavery,

00:14:12.850 --> 00:14:16.300
and we're 60 years into
affirmative action.

00:14:16.300 --> 00:14:18.550
And we've forgotten Martin
Luther King's content

00:14:18.550 --> 00:14:20.690
of our character rather
than the color of our skin.

00:14:20.690 --> 00:14:22.720
So we've got a whole
generation that

00:14:22.720 --> 00:14:26.290
grew up in an affluent, leisured
America, a multiracial America.

00:14:26.290 --> 00:14:29.170
But we're somehow looking
at the white middle class

00:14:29.170 --> 00:14:32.590
of the interior of the
country, and we're creating

00:14:32.590 --> 00:14:35.170
all these terms for them--

00:14:35.170 --> 00:14:40.420
Joe Biden's chumps,
dregs, Obama's clingers.

00:14:40.420 --> 00:14:43.910
And I guess it's just a
mechanism, as we said earlier,

00:14:43.910 --> 00:14:48.220
that you don't want to be around
people of a different class.

00:14:48.220 --> 00:14:50.750
Class is something
that's mobile and fluid.

00:14:50.750 --> 00:14:53.800
You and I are in the upper
middle, middle class.

00:14:53.800 --> 00:14:55.870
Tomorrow, we screw up.

00:14:55.870 --> 00:14:57.250
Our children don't make it.

00:14:57.250 --> 00:14:58.660
They can be poor.

00:14:58.660 --> 00:15:01.210
And so that was a
dilemma for Marx

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:05.050
when he looked at
states or nations that

00:15:05.050 --> 00:15:08.620
had a fluid upward mobility.

00:15:08.620 --> 00:15:10.370
Marxism never worked here.

00:15:10.370 --> 00:15:15.010
But once you calibrate race
into the equation, you say,

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:18.520
I postulate that everybody
who is non-white--

00:15:18.520 --> 00:15:21.010
we're going to get rid
of the old Black/white

00:15:21.010 --> 00:15:25.332
binary we were working on, the
legacy of Jim Crow or slavery--

00:15:25.332 --> 00:15:27.040
but now we're going
to create a new word.

00:15:27.040 --> 00:15:28.960
And this is mostly Barack Obama.

00:15:28.960 --> 00:15:30.940
It's called diversity.

00:15:30.940 --> 00:15:31.900
And guess what?

00:15:31.900 --> 00:15:36.070
You can be a Punjabi immigrant
and farm 10,000 acres,

00:15:36.070 --> 00:15:37.640
but you are not white.

00:15:37.640 --> 00:15:38.440
You're diverse.

00:15:38.440 --> 00:15:41.100
You can be an
Argentine aristocrat.

00:15:41.100 --> 00:15:42.850
And you can be blue-eyed
and blond-haired.

00:15:42.850 --> 00:15:44.620
But if you have a
Spanish-sounding name

00:15:44.620 --> 00:15:46.510
and trill your name
and your name is Tony

00:15:46.510 --> 00:15:51.050
and you call yourself
Antonio, then you are diverse.

00:15:51.050 --> 00:15:53.830
And that can lead to
careerist advantages.

00:15:53.830 --> 00:15:58.660
And it's a new binary, but it's
completely divorced from class.

00:15:58.660 --> 00:16:00.310
The idea of the
Civil Rights movement

00:16:00.310 --> 00:16:03.720
where African-Americans
been discriminated

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.610
so long and so
perniciously that it

00:16:06.610 --> 00:16:09.490
affected their
economic opportunity.

00:16:09.490 --> 00:16:15.320
That was what the EOC was, the
Office of Economic Opportunity.

00:16:15.320 --> 00:16:17.920
It's not that anymore.

00:16:17.920 --> 00:16:19.630
LeBron is a victim.

00:16:19.630 --> 00:16:21.850
Barack Obama is a victim.

00:16:21.850 --> 00:16:24.250
Anybody's a victim
if they can claim

00:16:24.250 --> 00:16:29.260
that they have some
meritorious, diverse background.

00:16:29.260 --> 00:16:31.810
This is why it was
very ironic for me,

00:16:31.810 --> 00:16:36.100
as I taught mostly minority
students my whole teaching life

00:16:36.100 --> 00:16:38.230
at California State, Fresno.

00:16:38.230 --> 00:16:42.530
And my message was Greek, Latin,
French, German literature,

00:16:42.530 --> 00:16:47.650
history, archaeology, art, and
your identity is irrelevant.

00:16:47.650 --> 00:16:50.350
And we're just going to
make you write and speak

00:16:50.350 --> 00:16:52.558
English better than people
on the East Coast and prep

00:16:52.558 --> 00:16:53.058
schools.

00:16:53.058 --> 00:16:55.330
That's what I would tell--
and I think we succeeded.

00:16:55.330 --> 00:16:58.225
We sent about 50 kids
to the Ivy League

00:16:58.225 --> 00:17:02.320
and professional schools,
classics or law, medicine.

00:17:02.320 --> 00:17:05.740
But some of the students that
I followed, it's very funny,

00:17:05.740 --> 00:17:07.849
they have retribalized.

00:17:07.849 --> 00:17:12.880
In other words, somebody I had,
if his name was Joe, he's José.

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:16.030
If his name was
Lopez, it's now López.

00:17:16.030 --> 00:17:19.270
And they are, even though
they don't speak Spanish,

00:17:19.270 --> 00:17:20.980
they're retribalizing
because they

00:17:20.980 --> 00:17:25.390
feel that the woke movement,
if they don't, they

00:17:25.390 --> 00:17:28.450
will be deemed inauthentic
and suffer career-wise,

00:17:28.450 --> 00:17:31.450
or if they do, they will
be rewarded commensurately.

00:17:31.450 --> 00:17:34.840
And that's really
dangerous, historically.

00:17:34.840 --> 00:17:36.580
That's what Hitler
did with the idea

00:17:36.580 --> 00:17:41.080
that, even if you speak German
and even if you live in Germany

00:17:41.080 --> 00:17:43.000
and even if you
follow German law,

00:17:43.000 --> 00:17:45.010
you're not really fully
German because we're

00:17:45.010 --> 00:17:46.690
going to identify
you by the blood

00:17:46.690 --> 00:17:49.330
and soil of pseudo-Aryanism.

00:17:49.330 --> 00:17:53.200
And it was one tribe
identifying in a way that

00:17:53.200 --> 00:17:55.402
transcended shared nationhood.

00:17:55.402 --> 00:17:56.860
And we're starting
to see that now.

00:17:56.860 --> 00:17:58.500
It's really scary.

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:00.040
What is the positive--

00:18:00.040 --> 00:18:01.600
I try not to be a
negative person,

00:18:01.600 --> 00:18:04.200
it's very easy to be
a negative person.

00:18:04.200 --> 00:18:05.560
What's the positive takeaway?

00:18:05.560 --> 00:18:07.630
If you look at
this country, what

00:18:07.630 --> 00:18:09.585
are some of the positive
aspects you can see?

00:18:09.585 --> 00:18:10.960
When I was 19, I
lived in Greece.

00:18:10.960 --> 00:18:12.340
And I lived there
two and a half years

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:13.757
and went all over
the Middle East.

00:18:13.757 --> 00:18:17.380
I have been to every country in
the Middle East, except Iran.

00:18:17.380 --> 00:18:18.430
And I look at the world--

00:18:18.430 --> 00:18:19.850
South America, Asia.

00:18:19.850 --> 00:18:23.860
And here we're sitting
in this Student Union.

00:18:23.860 --> 00:18:25.420
It's immaculate.

00:18:25.420 --> 00:18:27.490
Our coffee is not polluted.

00:18:27.490 --> 00:18:28.540
There's nobody listening.

00:18:28.540 --> 00:18:30.790
There's no device
that I know of.

00:18:30.790 --> 00:18:33.050
There's a freedom of expression.

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:36.440
And we take that for
granted, but it's very rare.

00:18:36.440 --> 00:18:38.200
And that's one thing.

00:18:38.200 --> 00:18:40.660
And then when you try to look
at that personal experience

00:18:40.660 --> 00:18:44.320
that we all share, that people
are not fighting or blowing up

00:18:44.320 --> 00:18:47.230
things, or they're not dragging
women off and putting them

00:18:47.230 --> 00:18:50.710
in a burqa, why is that?

00:18:50.710 --> 00:18:52.000
Why are we prosperous?

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:53.200
Why are we free?

00:18:53.200 --> 00:18:55.030
Well, we have this
constitutional system

00:18:55.030 --> 00:18:58.600
of 232 years that
solved the problem how

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:06.970
to divide judicial, executive,
and legislative power

00:19:06.970 --> 00:19:11.480
and checks and balances and
term limits for the president.

00:19:11.480 --> 00:19:15.580
And so the greatest threat
to any constitutional system

00:19:15.580 --> 00:19:17.450
is the absorption of power.

00:19:17.450 --> 00:19:20.620
We've stopped that.

00:19:20.620 --> 00:19:22.120
And we have a free market.

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:24.208
And we have too much
government, too much taxes,

00:19:24.208 --> 00:19:25.000
we've talked about.

00:19:25.000 --> 00:19:28.510
But we still are the
freest place in the world.

00:19:28.510 --> 00:19:31.540
And we're the only multiracial
democracy that's ever worked.

00:19:31.540 --> 00:19:33.850
Everybody says, well, the
United States is racist.

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:35.140
Nobody ever tries it.

00:19:35.140 --> 00:19:36.460
Brazil is the only one I know.

00:19:36.460 --> 00:19:37.543
It doesn't work very well.

00:19:37.543 --> 00:19:38.440
India can't do it.

00:19:38.440 --> 00:19:41.500
They've got all of these
caste systems and hatreds.

00:19:41.500 --> 00:19:44.200
Most countries are uniracial.

00:19:44.200 --> 00:19:46.598
If you and I decide tomorrow,
I want to be Japanese,

00:19:46.598 --> 00:19:48.640
let's go to Japan, they'll
never fully accept us.

00:19:48.640 --> 00:19:50.110
Because we don't look Japanese.

00:19:50.110 --> 00:19:51.250
Ditto China.

00:19:51.250 --> 00:19:53.200
Ditto Mexico.

00:19:53.200 --> 00:19:54.670
Ditto Tanzania.

00:19:54.670 --> 00:19:57.040
But this country has
redefined America

00:19:57.040 --> 00:20:00.160
not in the image of the
original settlers from Europe

00:20:00.160 --> 00:20:01.530
but as an idea.

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:04.050
That's a very rare
and powerful doctrine.

00:20:04.050 --> 00:20:07.593
If we get to the specifics,
as a classical historian

00:20:07.593 --> 00:20:09.510
or military historian,
I always say to myself,

00:20:09.510 --> 00:20:11.650
what makes a society powerful?

00:20:11.650 --> 00:20:14.260
How do they project
power beyond--

00:20:14.260 --> 00:20:17.640
and I don't mean just military--
cultural or political or

00:20:17.640 --> 00:20:18.660
social.

00:20:18.660 --> 00:20:20.260
There's only about six things.

00:20:20.260 --> 00:20:22.350
One is fuel-- transportation.

00:20:22.350 --> 00:20:24.630
In the horse age, do
they have big plains

00:20:24.630 --> 00:20:27.000
where horses can graze,
or they have wagons,

00:20:27.000 --> 00:20:30.480
or do they have
early steam engines?

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:33.418
In our case, we're the largest
producer of gas and oil

00:20:33.418 --> 00:20:33.960
in the world.

00:20:33.960 --> 00:20:36.150
I know that Joe Biden's
cut 3 million barrels off,

00:20:36.150 --> 00:20:38.250
but we still are.

00:20:38.250 --> 00:20:41.908
If you look at food, in terms
of actual dollar amount,

00:20:41.908 --> 00:20:43.950
we're the greatest producer
of food in the world.

00:20:43.950 --> 00:20:45.780
And we're by far
the most productive.

00:20:45.780 --> 00:20:47.848
China is going to have
trouble feeding itself,

00:20:47.848 --> 00:20:48.765
not the United States.

00:20:48.765 --> 00:20:52.410
The United States will never
have problems feeding itself.

00:20:52.410 --> 00:20:54.550
If you look at education,
if you look at, say,

00:20:54.550 --> 00:20:58.920
the Times Educational Supplement
or surveys that come out

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:01.710
of Japan-- not Americans--
and they look at the top 20

00:21:01.710 --> 00:21:05.460
or the top 30 universities--
and they don't do it in terms

00:21:05.460 --> 00:21:08.010
of their English department
or their gender studies,

00:21:08.010 --> 00:21:11.280
these studies are calibrated
on their medical schools,

00:21:11.280 --> 00:21:13.170
their law schools,
their business schools,

00:21:13.170 --> 00:21:17.310
their engineering, especially,
math, computer, science--

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:23.190
the United States usually
rates about 17 out of 20 or 28

00:21:23.190 --> 00:21:24.330
out of 30.

00:21:24.330 --> 00:21:27.090
And it's just predictable
when you read them.

00:21:27.090 --> 00:21:33.180
Caltech, MIT, Stanford,
Harvard, Yale, and then

00:21:33.180 --> 00:21:34.530
these big universities--

00:21:34.530 --> 00:21:38.250
Michigan or University
of Texas or UCLA.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:42.400
And that's pretty much all of
the universities in the world.

00:21:42.400 --> 00:21:46.050
So even for all of our criticism
of education, education

00:21:46.050 --> 00:21:48.030
we're there.

00:21:48.030 --> 00:21:48.720
So you got food.

00:21:48.720 --> 00:21:49.262
You got fuel.

00:21:49.262 --> 00:21:52.980
If you look at military, I'm a
big critic of the $800 billion.

00:21:52.980 --> 00:21:53.790
It's bloated.

00:21:53.790 --> 00:21:57.780
I think our generals and
admirals have become woke.

00:21:57.780 --> 00:22:00.270
But our military is the
most powerful military

00:22:00.270 --> 00:22:02.640
in the history of civilization.

00:22:02.640 --> 00:22:05.940
And you can calibrate
that by any--

00:22:05.940 --> 00:22:10.020
quality and number of
planes or aircraft carrier

00:22:10.020 --> 00:22:13.470
task forces or
submarine lethality.

00:22:13.470 --> 00:22:16.500
So it's just remarkable.

00:22:16.500 --> 00:22:20.590
And so when you look at
all-- and as I said earlier,

00:22:20.590 --> 00:22:22.630
we have the largest
economy in the world.

00:22:22.630 --> 00:22:24.648
And when you
calibrate that economy

00:22:24.648 --> 00:22:26.940
to the number of people
actually living in the country,

00:22:26.940 --> 00:22:28.350
it's pretty amazing.

00:22:28.350 --> 00:22:30.630
It's two or three
times more productive

00:22:30.630 --> 00:22:33.730
than China per person
in goods and services.

00:22:33.730 --> 00:22:35.730
So we have all the ingredients.

00:22:35.730 --> 00:22:39.600
And history says
to us our problem

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.360
is not we're not going to
eat, we're not going to defend

00:22:42.360 --> 00:22:43.770
ourselves, we can't educate.

00:22:43.770 --> 00:22:45.900
We do it so well that we
don't even think of it.

00:22:45.900 --> 00:22:47.100
We're on autopilot.

00:22:47.100 --> 00:22:48.673
And we don't replenish it.

00:22:48.673 --> 00:22:51.090
I think all we have to do is,
like we did during the Great

00:22:51.090 --> 00:22:53.310
Depression or on the
eve of World War II,

00:22:53.310 --> 00:22:56.370
get serious and
say, we're coasting.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:57.180
We're on the fumes.

00:22:57.180 --> 00:22:59.130
We've got to
recalibrate ourselves

00:22:59.130 --> 00:23:00.540
to what citizenship is.

00:23:00.540 --> 00:23:01.785
And we'll be fine.

00:23:01.785 --> 00:23:03.660
And we're going through
a cultural revolution

00:23:03.660 --> 00:23:06.090
right now, not like
Mao's but something

00:23:06.090 --> 00:23:08.140
that could be dangerous.

00:23:08.140 --> 00:23:10.500
And yet it's really
because all of us

00:23:10.500 --> 00:23:13.530
are so affluent and
leisured and blessed.

00:23:13.530 --> 00:23:15.810
And it's not out of
dearth or poverty.

00:23:15.810 --> 00:23:18.930
We should remember that
a lot of civilizations

00:23:18.930 --> 00:23:22.950
decline, or they vanish,
not from barbarians

00:23:22.950 --> 00:23:26.190
over the horizon or
they starve to death

00:23:26.190 --> 00:23:28.560
but because people
no longer believe

00:23:28.560 --> 00:23:30.390
they're better than
the alternative,

00:23:30.390 --> 00:23:33.630
or they believe you have
to be perfect to be good.

00:23:33.630 --> 00:23:37.590
And Americans just gotta
get back to the basic idea--

00:23:37.590 --> 00:23:39.390
we never made claims
that we're perfect,

00:23:39.390 --> 00:23:40.973
but we're better
than the alternative.

00:23:40.973 --> 00:23:42.710
And that's good enough.

00:23:42.710 --> 00:23:44.650
So I really like
that a lot, actually.

00:23:44.650 --> 00:23:45.400
I like that idea.

00:23:45.400 --> 00:23:48.110
So what are-- so you're
talking to a student,

00:23:48.110 --> 00:23:49.770
20-something years old--

00:23:49.770 --> 00:23:53.910
what are practical, if you were
to lay out Dr. Hanson's game

00:23:53.910 --> 00:23:55.860
plan for somebody, what
are practical steps

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:56.677
that every day--

00:23:56.677 --> 00:23:57.427
For a 20-year-old?

00:23:57.427 --> 00:23:58.710
Well, 21-year-old.

00:23:58.710 --> 00:24:03.030
But practical steps every person
can take to make a difference.

00:24:03.030 --> 00:24:05.910
I think the problem we
have with 20-year-olds is

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:08.460
they have been sold
a bill of goods

00:24:08.460 --> 00:24:12.750
that they have to be cosmically
moral or cosmically politically

00:24:12.750 --> 00:24:16.080
correct or cosmically woke.

00:24:16.080 --> 00:24:18.270
And they neglect things
that are the ingredients

00:24:18.270 --> 00:24:21.630
of a successful and
productive citizenship.

00:24:21.630 --> 00:24:23.820
So every 20-year-old
should say to themselves,

00:24:23.820 --> 00:24:25.710
am I self-sufficient?

00:24:25.710 --> 00:24:26.950
Am I polite to people?

00:24:26.950 --> 00:24:29.670
Do I follow the
rules of civility?

00:24:29.670 --> 00:24:31.410
Am I fair to people?

00:24:31.410 --> 00:24:34.920
Do I judge people on the
content of their character?

00:24:34.920 --> 00:24:39.330
And then they have to say,
am I autonomous, as much

00:24:39.330 --> 00:24:40.500
as a 20-year-old can be?

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:42.460
I do not want to be
dependent on people.

00:24:42.460 --> 00:24:43.710
I want to be productive.

00:24:43.710 --> 00:24:47.100
I want to be the person
people come to for advice.

00:24:47.100 --> 00:24:50.130
I do not want to go to other
people for subsistence.

00:24:50.130 --> 00:24:54.270
That was an idea that
Jefferson and what Tocqueville

00:24:54.270 --> 00:24:56.220
saw, that what made
America work, where

00:24:56.220 --> 00:24:57.720
they're the yeoman farmer.

00:24:57.720 --> 00:25:00.350
And they combined their
mind with their body.

00:25:00.350 --> 00:25:02.210
And they were not
a dependent class.

00:25:02.210 --> 00:25:05.720
And Tocqueville even talks
about prolonged adolescence.

00:25:05.720 --> 00:25:08.270
You don't want to live in
your parents' basement.

00:25:08.270 --> 00:25:11.720
At some point, you've got to
say, I'm going to be a citizen.

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:14.360
And that citizen requires
that most-- not all,

00:25:14.360 --> 00:25:17.180
we're not demanding everybody--
but most citizens are going

00:25:17.180 --> 00:25:18.290
to marry, they're
going to have children,

00:25:18.290 --> 00:25:19.790
they're going to
buy a home, they're

00:25:19.790 --> 00:25:22.040
going to be self-sufficient
and, at a very early age,

00:25:22.040 --> 00:25:24.630
a person say that's
my trajectory.

00:25:24.630 --> 00:25:28.680
And it's kind of an
attitude of do no harm.

00:25:28.680 --> 00:25:33.350
But if you do the opposite,
if your own life is a mess

00:25:33.350 --> 00:25:36.110
and you're not able to
take care of yourself

00:25:36.110 --> 00:25:39.890
but you're judging people
all the way across the world,

00:25:39.890 --> 00:25:42.172
or you're living in Palo
Alto and you're saying,

00:25:42.172 --> 00:25:44.630
I can't stand those people in
Mississippi that you've never

00:25:44.630 --> 00:25:47.810
seen, then you're
cosmically woke,

00:25:47.810 --> 00:25:50.250
but you're personally
a liability.

00:25:50.250 --> 00:25:53.180
And so that, I think, is the
biggest problem that I have

00:25:53.180 --> 00:25:55.070
when I see young people.

00:25:55.070 --> 00:25:58.100
Because I'm a fellow
at Stanford University.

00:25:58.100 --> 00:26:02.090
I don't teach anymore
there, but I do interact

00:26:02.090 --> 00:26:03.380
with a lot of students.

00:26:03.380 --> 00:26:07.400
And they have this
misguided notion

00:26:07.400 --> 00:26:09.620
that if they have
a high test score

00:26:09.620 --> 00:26:11.180
or they lived in
the right zip code

00:26:11.180 --> 00:26:13.340
or they drive the
right car then that

00:26:13.340 --> 00:26:17.690
gives them sort of an exemption
from concrete reality.

00:26:17.690 --> 00:26:19.320
And they don't have any balance.

00:26:19.320 --> 00:26:22.040
So if you say to
an Ivy League kid,

00:26:22.040 --> 00:26:25.910
who is the epitome-- what would
you do if the lights went out?

00:26:25.910 --> 00:26:28.370
Do you know how to
change a circuit breaker?

00:26:28.370 --> 00:26:30.140
Do you know how
to use a chainsaw?

00:26:30.140 --> 00:26:33.320
These are all skills that the
average American excelled at.

00:26:33.320 --> 00:26:38.210
When George Patton raced
through Europe from August 1

00:26:38.210 --> 00:26:41.360
to September 5, let's say,
of 1944 in the Third Army,

00:26:41.360 --> 00:26:44.012
people were curious,
how did he do it--

00:26:44.012 --> 00:26:45.470
the occupied French
that were being

00:26:45.470 --> 00:26:47.510
liberated, the British,
even other Americans.

00:26:47.510 --> 00:26:49.040
He said, it's very simple.

00:26:49.040 --> 00:26:51.830
American kids grow
up fixing things.

00:26:51.830 --> 00:26:54.680
When our Shermans break down,
we don't send the transmission

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:55.910
back to the factory.

00:26:55.910 --> 00:26:59.120
It's not fine-tuned by
some German technician.

00:26:59.120 --> 00:27:02.030
We get the kids that
worked on their model As.

00:27:02.030 --> 00:27:03.530
They love taking things apart.

00:27:03.530 --> 00:27:05.390
That Sherman is fixed.

00:27:05.390 --> 00:27:07.640
They're crawling over it,
and they're go, go, go.

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:09.290
And then when we
hit the hedgerows,

00:27:09.290 --> 00:27:10.680
they want to make Rhinos.

00:27:10.680 --> 00:27:12.060
They adapt.

00:27:12.060 --> 00:27:13.550
So what he was
basically saying is

00:27:13.550 --> 00:27:16.580
that that generation that
came out of the Depression

00:27:16.580 --> 00:27:19.370
was very versatile, and
they were very imaginative.

00:27:19.370 --> 00:27:20.960
And they were very mechanical.

00:27:20.960 --> 00:27:21.840
And we've lost that.

00:27:21.840 --> 00:27:23.840
I think we've got to go
back to the idea there's

00:27:23.840 --> 00:27:26.990
going to be a lot of
people in this country who

00:27:26.990 --> 00:27:28.370
don't need to go to college.

00:27:28.370 --> 00:27:31.250
They can get a liberal
arts education on YouTube.

00:27:31.250 --> 00:27:33.140
They can get it on the internet.

00:27:33.140 --> 00:27:39.368
But they can be well compensated
with vocational skills.

00:27:39.368 --> 00:27:40.910
There's nothing more
impressive to me

00:27:40.910 --> 00:27:45.320
than a master cement person
or electrician or a plumber.

00:27:45.320 --> 00:27:47.900
And I try to do my own plumbing.

00:27:47.900 --> 00:27:52.250
But when I get outfoxed
by ancient pipes

00:27:52.250 --> 00:27:55.580
and I call a guy in, it's
like watching a maestro

00:27:55.580 --> 00:27:56.840
at an orchestra.

00:27:56.840 --> 00:27:59.540
And he's rewarded
far better than most

00:27:59.540 --> 00:28:00.690
of the professors I know.

00:28:00.690 --> 00:28:02.870
And what is wrong
with what he's doing?

00:28:02.870 --> 00:28:06.530
So I know that in the
'30s and '20s and '40s,

00:28:06.530 --> 00:28:09.200
we got the idea of
upward mobility BA,

00:28:09.200 --> 00:28:12.230
but I think in the new
postmodern 21st century,

00:28:12.230 --> 00:28:15.560
it's going to be, let's forget
the titles and the alphabet

00:28:15.560 --> 00:28:18.140
soup that follows your name and
look at what you can actually

00:28:18.140 --> 00:28:19.160
do.

00:28:19.160 --> 00:28:20.930
And I think that's exciting.

00:28:20.930 --> 00:28:21.450
Is that it?

00:28:21.450 --> 00:28:21.950
Yes, sir.

00:28:21.950 --> 00:28:22.685
I think so.

00:28:22.685 --> 00:28:23.840
Hey, that was good, Nick.

00:28:23.840 --> 00:28:24.340
Thank you.

00:28:24.340 --> 00:28:24.600
Thank you.

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:25.495
It was nice to meet you.

00:28:25.495 --> 00:28:26.453
Yeah, nice to meet you.

00:28:26.453 --> 00:28:29.200
[MUSIC PLAYING]

